<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ludwig I of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1786">1786</a>–<a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ludwig I was king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> from 1825 to 1848. A patron of art and classical architecture, he rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> with buildings such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glyptothek">Glyptothek</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwigstrasse">Ludwigstrasse</a> and moved the university there. His son Otto became the first king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. His affair with the dancer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lola-montez">Lola Montez</a> and the revolutions of 1848 forced him to abdicate in favour of his son Maximilian II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ludwig-i-of-bavaria.jpg" alt="Ludwig I of Bavaria" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Karl Stieler · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Karl_Stieler_-_King_Ludwig_I_in_his_Coronation_Robes_-_WGA21796.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
